\DOC LOCAL_MUL_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges a subroutine call to a modular-multiplication routine inside an
{eventually} goal, advancing the symbolic machine state by one step and
replacing the state-describing assumptions with ones about the new state.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes one {int} argument, {n}, the index of the new symbolic state;
it is used to name the state variable mentioned by the resulting assumptions
and by the goal's {eventually} argument. The goal is always of the form
{A ?- eventually ... s}, where {A} contains a block {S(s)} of assumptions
describing the state {s}: code loaded, register and memory contents, program
counter, and an accumulated MAYCHANGE relation from the initial state. The new
block {S'(s')} for the successor state {s'} has the program counter set to the
post-call address, the destination memory block's contents replaced by the
product of the two argument blocks reduced modulo the prime, the assumptions
for registers holding the now-consumed call arguments dropped, and the
MAYCHANGE relation re-stated for the new state. Other assumptions in {A}, such
as the nonoverlapping and arithmetic side conditions, are left untouched.
{
   A, S(s) ?- eventually P s
   ========================== LOCAL_MUL_TAC n
   A, S'(s') ?- eventually P s'
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, on goals of
this shape.

\ENDDOC
